{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794522344,"nonce":"yE0K0dq1wASIg4EjHzN4GWuEkMw=","message":"294c018 closes the first open point: hxn_future::together(|| { ... }) runs a scope whose changes reach the host as one. publish() holds back the tags the scope's changes post (thread-local list) and the outermost scope adds them to the queue in one step when it ends, by returning or by panicking; a library-wide RwLock is held for writing during the scope and for reading in AnySignal::get, so the host never reads a signal's value mid-scope. Since an inbox drains the whole queue before effects run, effects never see one of two values set together changed and the other not. Nested scopes are part of the outer one; scopes on different threads run one at a time; the library's own Signal::get never waits.\n\nTested in the outbox test: posting is held back until the scope ends, and a host thread draining the queue while another thread sets two signals together 2000 times always holds equal values after each drain; with together replaced by a plain call the check fails 8 runs of 8. Passed 11 of 11 runs, and under Miri (20 rounds). All eleven counter modes and the examples on Ash pass. Not used by any generated code or example yet; it is Rust API for library authors.\n\nStill open: deliveries happen on one thread (the one that first asked, or the one calling sync); effects rerun at the end of a delivery; a stream's place in the order is that of its first unread item. These are how delivery is designed rather than gaps, so this issue could be closed once those are written up as the design.","files":null}]}